# 30. Docker Runtime Privileges

Containers normally run with restricted capabilities compared with the host.

Run container with privileged mode:

```bash
docker run --privileged myimage
```

This gives the container significantly broader access to host devices/capabilities.

Because of the security implications, `--privileged` should be used only when required.

Other useful security/runtime options include:

```bash
docker run --user 1000:1000 myimage
```

Run as a particular user.

```bash
docker run --read-only myimage
```

Make the container root filesystem read-only.

```bash
docker run --cap-drop ALL myimage
```

Drop Linux capabilities.

```bash
docker run --cap-add NET_ADMIN myimage
```

Add a specific capability.

