# 49. Docker Runtime Resource Management

By default, containers do not have a fixed CPU/memory limit.

Resource limits can be specified using `docker run`.

---

## Memory limit

```bash
docker run --memory=512m nginx
```

Short form:

```bash
docker run -m 512m nginx
```

Example:

```bash
docker run --memory=1g myimage
```

---

## Memory reservation

```bash
docker run --memory-reservation=256m myimage
```

---

## Memory + swap

Example:

```bash
docker run --memory=512m --memory-swap=1g myimage
```

---

## CPU limit

```bash
docker run --cpus=1 myimage
```

Meaning approximately one CPU's worth of CPU time.

Example:

```bash
docker run --cpus=0.5 myimage
```

Limits container to approximately half a CPU.

---

## CPU shares

```bash
docker run --cpu-shares=512 myimage
```

Default weight is generally 1024.

CPU shares matter when CPU resources are constrained.

---

## Specific CPU cores

```bash
docker run --cpuset-cpus="0,1" myimage
```

or:

```bash
docker run --cpuset-cpus="0-3" myimage
```

---

## Check resource usage

```bash
docker stats
```

One-time:

```bash
docker stats --no-stream
```

The resource documentation covers memory limits, CPU limits, CPU shares, CPU sets and related runtime controls.

